---
import GravityFoldFlight from "../../../components/studies/GravityFoldFlight.svelte";
import GravityFoldVisibility from "../../../components/studies/GravityFoldVisibility.svelte";
import GravityFxFizzles from "../../../components/studies/GravityFxFizzles.svelte";
import GravityFxMotion from "../../../components/studies/GravityFxMotion.svelte";
import GravityFxOverlap from "../../../components/studies/GravityFxOverlap.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
	(entry) => entry.slug === "gravity-coil-fx-2026-11-03",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section>
		<h2>01 / Making gravity become potential</h2>
		<p>
			A ship passes a planet while its engines fire. The gravity coil begins
			working: gravitational pull becomes potential, expressed as an exotic
			physics light show. That is the intended fiction; what starts the
			conversion, whether it changes the ship’s pull, and how it ends remain
			questions at the start of this study. Then, the fitted coil’s static form
			was established; collection and its visual behaviour were not implemented.
			Parts 03–04 record the accepted behavior and delivery scope.
		</p>
		<p>
			The effect should make the process feel extraordinary while giving its
			activity a readable shape in time. An onset, sustained conversion and
			ending may need different expressions. Engine FX already describe thrust
			and leave fading residue behind a moving ship. Sharing the FX layer means
			considering overlap, brightness, direction and persistence together:
			turning or coasting should still make sense when both processes are
			visible. The coil’s placement above or below the hull also matters to
			where the light can appear to originate. When both effects occupy the same
			pixel, the compositing rule must also be decided: addition, burn, blending
			or replacement.
		</p>
		<p>
			This cycle aims to settle one coherent visual behaviour for conversion:
			when it appears, what moves or changes, and how it composes with engine FX
			at flight scale. The relationship between planet, coil and potential is
			the central uncertainty to explore. Trigger and intensity scenarios can be
			provisional while their meaning is investigated; exact resource balance,
			storage artwork and redesigning the engines are outside this cycle.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>02 / The light show in a shared FX layer</h2>
		<details>
			<summary id="overlap">
				02.1 / Two effects, one pixel — Add selected
			</summary>
			<p>
				<strong>Selected: A / Add.</strong>
				Coil and engine FX add their light where they share a pixel, using the
				comparison’s Canvas
				<code>lighter</code>
				compositing rule on the transparent FX surface. This is the overlap rule
				carried into subsequent exploration. The five comparisons remain below
				as the reviewed reference.
			</p>
			<p>
				The selection settles compositing, not the violet fixture’s colour,
				shape or opacity. Brightness under repeated emissions, flight-scale
				readability and the conversion’s onset, movement and ending still need
				investigation. Production rendering is unchanged.
			</p>
			<p>
				These five comparisons use the same production Light ion beam painter
				and a violet elliptical field fixture. Move the field across the beam,
				vary its opacity and reverse the draw order. The field is a test mark,
				not a proposed coil shape or accepted colour. This isolates the overlap
				rule before choosing the conversion’s choreography.
			</p>
			<GravityFxOverlap client:load />
			<p>
				Add and Screen test luminous coexistence; alpha blending tests a
				readable foreground effect; Burn tests a field that disrupts light;
				Override tests exclusive ownership of the pixel. Compare the crossing
				with the untouched beam and field, especially when the engine is drawn
				last.
			</p>
			<p>
				The two marks are composed on a transparent FX surface over the void.
				Burn therefore acts where the marks overlap, not against an opaque black
				background. Override replaces pixels above a 5% source-alpha threshold
				with opaque colour and ignores the opacity control for the later mark.
				This is a deliberately hard replacement rule, not Canvas’s whole-surface
				copy.
			</p>
			<p>
				This enlarged, static fixture establishes neither flight-scale
				readability nor accumulation across many emissions. Trigger, duration,
				planet connection, and whether conversion harvests or distorts gravity
				remain open.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section>
		<details>
			<summary id="conversion">02.2 / What does conversion do?</summary>
			<p>
				Gather and Fold remain useful motion references. Capture was rejected
				and removed. The line treatment is not carried forward; the next round
				explores pixel fizzles and static, including residue left by invisible
				collection. All use the accepted Add rule, the production coil artwork
				and the production Light ion beam painter. The violet light remains
				provisional. Compare the sense of intake and completion, then switch the
				engine off to see what its light conceals.
			</p>
			<GravityFxMotion client:load />
			<p>
				Shared eight-second fixture: idle until 1 s, engagement from 1–2 s,
				conversion until 5 s, release until 6.5 s, then idle. Playback starts on
				request and stops at 8 s; the slider scrubs the same deterministic
				sequence. No randomness or simulated resource gain. The field direction
				is a cue, not a planet tether. The engine stays lit to expose overlap
				during release.
			</p>
			<p>
				This is an enlarged equipment bench: coil artwork at four times its
				fitted size, with a nearby engine mark deliberately crossing the effect.
				It does not represent a ship mount, flight motion, or actual plume
				accumulation. These sketches investigate visual meaning before testing
				the chosen behaviour on a moving, fully fitted ship. Timing, trigger
				conditions and whether collection changes gravitational pull remain
				provisional.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section>
		<details>
			<summary id="fizzles">02.3 / The space left behind — E retained</summary>
			<p>
				<strong>E / Granular fold is the chosen motion direction.</strong>
				Its shifting pixel volume is promising, but too faint. The next
				comparison moves it away from the field origin and increases visibility.
				This selects the idea, not its current brightness, size, colour or
				timing.
			</p>
			<p>
				Conversion may be invisible while the disturbed space around it gives it
				away. Four examples replace drawn lines with many small pixel fizzles. D
				gathers from the incoming field; E keeps the fold’s local movement; F
				disperses residue beyond the device; G leaves static in the region an
				unseen gathering front has crossed. “Behind” here follows the field’s
				direction, independently of a ship’s eventual direction of travel.
			</p>
			<GravityFxFizzles client:load />
			<p>
				All four retain Add, the same provisional violet palette, the real coil
				artwork and the nearby ion mark. Emissions begin at 1 s and stop at 5 s;
				existing fizzles live for 0.7–1.5 s, leaving a visible aftermath before
				the field clears. The eight-second replay and scrubber share fixed
				particle births and deterministic noise. Engine FX can be switched off.
			</p>
			<p>
				Look for whether the activity feels like matter arriving, a local fold,
				or space reacting after something has happened. These remain enlarged
				equipment sketches; field direction is illustrative, and neither flight
				scale nor a moving ship’s wake has been tested. Add is settled; the
				texture, colour, density, timing and gravitational consequences are
				open.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h3 id="offset-fold">02.4 / A dispersed fold downstream</h3>
		<p>
			The dense and coarse bright proposals were rejected: both lost E’s
			character and felt too condensed. Visibility should come from a dispersed,
			softer disturbance with dim edges, not packing more bright grains into its
			centre. E remains the reference; H and I have been removed.
		</p>
		<GravityFoldVisibility client:load />
		<p>
			J’s Colour and Top brightness sliders adjust its live palette and light
			intensity. 273° and 100% reproduce the starting treatment; Reset J
			restores them. Values are exploratory and apply only to J in this open
			page.
		</p>
		<p>
			Left: E’s original grain, palette, emission rate and motion translated
			away from the field origin. Right: J spreads that same emission rate
			across a wider volume, softens each grain and fades the outer region into
			space. Neither uses the rejected one-pixel minimum at flight scale. This
			isolates spatial softness from simply increasing brightness or density.
		</p>
		<p>
			Both centres remain 15 ship drawing units downstream of the coil; field
			origin is left. Add and continuous playback stay, with Fold FX off by
			default. Switching off clears the comparison. The lower samples show 0.65
			flight scale at 100% browser zoom; full ships and moving camera still need
			review. Flight-scale visibility remains an investigation, not a solved
			result.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h3 id="planet-flight">02.5 / J in a powered flyby</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Selected trial: two purple J folds at 273° and 250%.</strong>
			Each fold is twice the width and height, with its original centre distance
			of 15 ship drawing units restored. The larger size provides the desired
			coverage without moving the effect closer to its coil. The soft edges and
			brightness stay; independent grain patterns overlap using Add. Press Fly
			by for a repeating powered pass: approach from outside the planet’s
			influence, cross the field, then leave it. Both engines drive the
			approach; from 1–1.6 seconds the left engine alone turns the ship inside
			the field, then both resume. Watch the coils rotate with the hull while
			their folds keep pointing away from the planet. Turn off Turn in field to
			compare the straight pass. Game camera uses the production framing; switch
			it off for a close view, or use ¼ speed to inspect the pass.
		</p>
		<GravityFoldFlight client:load />
		<p>
			Add is active, but its visible contribution remains under review. Sparse
			grains only brighten where pixels overlap; the previous mint palette
			offered little hue contrast, and already-bright engine cores can saturate.
			This purple trial restores colour contrast with the mint engine trails.
			Pause inside the field and toggle Add fold light to compare addition with
			normal transparency on the same frame. This comparison keeps the selected
			colour, brightness, size and distance unchanged.
		</p>
		<p>
			This repeatable fixture starts the fitted Fighter 350 units left and 140
			above the first planet, moving right at 60 units/s with heading zero. Each
			three-second pass runs the real simulation with stock gravity, moving
			planets, engine response, power drain and collisions. Both hull slots
			carry gravity coils, replacing the starter battery; starter engines and
			the ship’s base power capacity remain. Repeating resets the run; disable
			Repeat flyby to continue, or Powered flyby to fly manually. Engine
			brightness responds to the game’s actual delivered output even while the
			controls remain held.
		</p>
		<p>
			Implemented collection: each coil adds up to 1 energy/second inside any
			planet’s influence, limited by storage capacity. Wells do not multiply the
			rate. The shared game renderer points folds away from the nearest well and
			fades them over 0.25 seconds when collection stops. The readout shows
			actual energy deposited. Engine marks and J add on a transparent surface
			above the ship. The optional close view is a study inspection camera.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>03 / Ready for an alpha trial</h2>
		<p>
			The approved treatment is J: purple 273°, 250% brightness, twice the
			original width and height, soft dispersed edges, and the original 15-unit
			offset away from the planet. Each fitted coil has independent grain; light
			adds on the shared engine FX surface. The two-coil turning flyby is the
			accepted visual reference.
		</p>
		<p>
			Each fitted coil automatically generates 1 energy/second inside any
			planet’s influence, during thrust or coasting. Coils stack; overlapping
			wells do not. Storage capacity caps collection. Gravity and motion are
			unchanged. FX follow actual collection and fade out when storage is full
			or the ship leaves the field.
		</p>
		<p>
			The simulation already owns planet influence, fitted equipment, fixed
			substeps and capacity-limited power. The renderer already provides engine
			trails and mount transforms. Required additions are collection accounting,
			a shared fold renderer and an activity fade. No dependencies, storage
			migration or physics-constant changes are needed.
		</p>
		<p>
			There are no alpha implementation blockers. Baseline consumption is 3
			energy/second before engine draw: one coil offsets one third, two offset
			two thirds, before solar charging. This is an endurance benefit, not
			unlimited propulsion. The 1/s rate remains a balancing hypothesis. A
			0.25-second fade and nearest influencing planet for visual direction are
			implementation choices to review in play; neither changes the collection
			rate.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>04 / Deliver collection and shared flight FX</h2>
		<p>
			No experience-principle or art-direction change is proposed for
			docs/design.md. The accepted behavior is recorded in
			docs/history/ship-builder.md. Starter loadouts remain unchanged; the part
			is fitted through the existing builder.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				Small: add a validated collection definition and deterministic power
				accounting. Verify inside/outside boundaries, multiple coils,
				overlapping wells, capacity, thrust/coasting and unchanged motion.
			</li>
			<li>
				Medium, after collection: extract the accepted fold into shared flight
				rendering, drive its fade from actual collection, and compose it with
				engine FX on a transparent additive surface. Use it in the game and the
				flyby; preserve the earlier exploration drawings as references.
			</li>
			<li>
				Small, after integration: update catalog text and the living
				specification, expose live collection in the flyby, and verify types,
				contracts, tests and browser behavior. Human balance and sustained
				device-performance review remain separate from automated correctness.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			The design is accepted and implementation is authorized. Scope excludes
			changing starter fleets, gravity strength, engine tuning, storage artwork
			or solar Loading coil behavior.
		</p>
	</section>
	<p>
		<a href="/lab/reviews/gravity-coil-fx-2026-11-03"
			>Study-quality review and implementation evidence</a
		>
	</p>
</StudyLayout>
